    stimulus: "Religious practices are the sacred threads that weave together adherents' beliefs and connect them to the divine.",
    question: "With reference to the statement, analyse how the significant practice of Death and Mourning expresses the beliefs of Judaism.",
    questionNote: "This is NOT a genuine NESA Section III question — every Judaism question in this pack is essay-length, so this pack's chosen significant practice (Death & Mourning) gets essay-length practice too. Claude adapted it by extending the real 2024 HSC Section II Q16(b) (8 marks, using this same genuine stimulus) and the real 2025 HSC Section II Q16(a) (3 marks) into a full Section III-style, 20-mark essay. The stimulus and underlying question focus are genuine NESA content; the essay-length criteria and model answer below are self-authored by Claude to match real Section III conventions, not verbatim NESA content.",
    criteria: [
      { marks: "17–20", desc: ["Presents a comprehensive and accurate analysis of how the significant practice of Death and Mourning expresses the beliefs of Judaism", "Integrates the statement effectively throughout the response", "Supports a cohesive and well-structured response with detailed, relevant and accurate information", "Integrates relevant and accurate terminology"] },
      { marks: "13–16", desc: ["Presents an accurate analysis", "Integrates the statement within the response", "Supports a structured response with relevant and accurate information", "Uses relevant and accurate terminology"] },
      { marks: "9–12", desc: ["Presents some analysis", "Refers to the statement within the response", "Supports the response with relevant information", "Uses accurate terminology"] },
      { marks: "5–8", desc: ["Makes general statements about how Death and Mourning expresses the beliefs of Judaism", "May refer to the statement", "Uses some examples and/or terminology"] },
      { marks: "1–4", desc: ["Makes general statements about Judaism", "May use some terminology"] }
    ],
    criteriaNote: "Self-authored by Claude, adapting the real 2024/2025 NESA Section II marking-guideline language for this topic into Section III-style 5-band criteria (matching the real 20-mark Section III bands used elsewhere in this pack) — not verbatim NESA Section III criteria, since NESA has not set Death & Mourning as a genuine Section III essay question in recent papers.",
    modelAnswer: {
      label: "Model essay answer (written by Claude — not an official NESA answer)",
      text: "Death and mourning are the sacred threads weaving together Judaism's core beliefs — in One God, divinely inspired law, and Covenant — while connecting adherents to the divine at life's most significant moment. The practice is grounded in halakhah, the collective body of Jewish law drawn from the Written Torah (Tanakh) and Oral Torah (including the Talmud), which structures every stage of dying, burial and mourning through the mitzvot Kavod Hamet (honouring the dead) and Nichum Aveilim (comforting mourners): “For dust you are and to dust you shall return” (Genesis 3:19).\n\nBelief in One God is affirmed most clearly at the moment of death itself. The gosses (the dying person) is supported to recite the Shema as their central declaration of faith: “Hear O Israel, the Lord is our God, the Lord is One” (Deuteronomy 6:4). This same belief is then carried through the entire mourning period by the Kaddish, Judaism's central prayer, recited daily by mourners. Strikingly, the Kaddish never once mentions death; instead it proclaims God's greatness (“Magnified and sanctified be His great name…”), so that reciting it affirms belief in One God even while a mourner's own faith is being tested by grief. “God created humankind in His image” (Genesis 1:27, Tzelem Elohim — a belief rather than a mitzvah, and worth stating precisely as such) is reflected in the deliberate plainness of Jewish funeral customs: a simple, uniform shroud and coffin symbolise that all people, rich or poor, are equal before God, while readings such as Job 1:21 (“Naked I came from my mother's womb… blessed be the name of the Lord”) reinforce humility in the face of the divine.\n\nThe practice is equally shaped by belief in divinely inspired law and Covenant. “You shall not desecrate the dead” (Deuteronomy 21:23) underpins Kavod Hamet, and is fulfilled practically when a Chevra Kadisha (a burial volunteer group) performs taharah, the ritual purification of the body, generally within 24 hours of death. “Comfort the mourners” (drawn from Isaiah 40:1 and rabbinic tradition) underpins Nichum Aveilim, structured through clearly defined stages: Shiva, the intense first seven days, during which mourners do not leave home, work or engage in entertainment, sit on low chairs and wear keriah (torn clothing); Shloshim, extending to thirty days, during which mourners return to work but avoid music and celebrations; and, for the loss of a parent, Avelut, lasting twelve months in total. Each stage gradually reintegrates the mourner into ordinary life while never letting go of halakhic structure. Orthodox Judaism observes these restrictions strictly, while Progressive Judaism often relaxes elements of them — for instance allowing a mourner to return to work earlier during Shiva — showing that the same underlying law can be lived out with real diversity across the streams while still expressing obedience to it. Covenant is expressed even more directly: the deceased is buried in their tallit (prayer shawl) with its tzitzit strings deliberately cut, symbolising the fulfilment of the 613 mitzvot, and, outside Israel, soil from Israel is placed on the coffin, recalling God's covenantal land-promise to Abraham — “the Lord made a covenant with Abram… to your descendants I give this land” (Genesis 15:18). The Kaddish itself reinforces this, its words affirming that God will “establish His kingdom”, tying personal grief back to the covenantal relationship between God and His people.\n\nFinally, Death and Mourning is deeply significant for both the individual and the Jewish community, in ways that show it to be a living, adaptable practice rather than a fixed ritual performed by rote. For the individual, burial within 24 hours anticipates the Jewish hope of resurrection at the end of days — “Many who sleep in the dust will awake” (Daniel 12:2) — which Orthodox and Conservative Judaism tend to read as a literal, future hope, while Progressive Judaism more often interprets it metaphorically, as spiritual continuity beyond death; either way, the ritual structure gives the individual mourner a clear, supported path through grief. For the community, the requirement that Kaddish be recited within a minyan — a quorum of at least ten adults, traditionally men in Orthodox and most Conservative settings, though Progressive minyanim may include women — turns private loss into shared, communal responsibility, fulfilling the ethical teachings of Tikkun Olam (repairing the world) and Gemilut Chasadim (acts of loving-kindness) and ensuring that no mourner grieves alone. This communal, covenantal character has shown itself to be genuinely dynamic in the modern world: during COVID-19 lockdowns, many synagogues organised “Zoom minyanim” so mourners could still recite Kaddish in community, and in Israel livestreamed funerals let family members abroad remain meaningfully present. Variation across the streams — Orthodox and Conservative funerals kept deliberately uniform, with no flowers and no individual booklet, against some Progressive communities now permitting eco-burials or cremation as an expression of personal autonomy and environmental concern, while still retaining core rituals such as Kaddish — shows that Death and Mourning continues to weave together belief in One God, divinely inspired law and Covenant precisely by adapting its outward form to a changing modern context without losing its underlying religious meaning, exactly as the statement describes."
    }
