    criteriaNote: "Captured verbatim from the genuine 2024 NESA HSC Studies of Religion Marking Guidelines.",
    modelAnswer: {
      label: "Model answer on Jewish Feminism (condensed essay, written by Claude to match this real question — not an official NESA answer)",
      text: "Jewish Feminism has been a genuine agent of change, enriching Judaism's faith and inspiring new forms of religious expression across every stream, though unevenly and at each stream's own pace. It is best understood not as a rejection of Judaism but as a modern school of thought that applies Tikkun Olam (repairing the world — not itself one of the 613 mitzvot, but a guiding ethical principle grounded in mitzvot such as Tzedakah, Pikuach Nefesh, Gemilut Chasadim and Bal Tashchit) to challenge patriarchal structures and promote gender equity. Its theological foundation is Tzelem Elohim: “So God created mankind in His image, male and female He created them” (Genesis 1:27), which grounds the claim that gender equity fulfils, rather than threatens, covenantal responsibility — giving the movement real theological force alongside its social one.\n\nIts impact on the development of Judaism is clearest in leadership and education. In 1972, ten Jewish feminists formed Ezrat Nashim, producing “The Call for Change”, a founding document seeking spiritual, ritual, intellectual and political opportunities for women; the movement points to Numbers 27:1–7, where the daughters of Zelophehad successfully petition Moses for inheritance rights (“The daughters of Zelophehad speak rightly”), as genuine scriptural precedent for women shaping halakhah rather than simply being subject to it. Ordination has changed dramatically across the twentieth and twenty-first centuries: Regina Jonas, ordained in Berlin in 1935, was the world's first ordained female rabbi, serving the city's liberal Jewish community through the Nazi era before being murdered in Auschwitz — a reminder that the movement's history is not only one of steady progress. Progressive Judaism ordained its first American woman rabbi, Sally Priesand, in Cincinnati in 1972; Conservative Judaism followed with its 1983 decision to permit women's ordination, and Rabbi Amy Eilberg became its first ordained woman in New York in 1985. Even Orthodoxy, which holds most tightly to traditional limits, has changed incrementally: JOFA (the Jewish Orthodox Feminist Alliance), founded in 1997 by Blu Greenburg, advocates for women's meaningful participation “within the framework of halachah”, and Orthodox rabbanits — including Rabbanit Sara Hurwitz (2009) and Rabbanit Ellyse Borghi (Melbourne, 2019) — can now read Torah publicly and preach, even though their role remains halakhically limited, for instance excluded from leading mixed minyanim. Education has shifted too: most Jewish schools in Sydney are now co-educational, applying “teach them diligently to your children” (Deuteronomy 6:7) inclusively, with the movement arguing that expanding access to Torah study strengthens covenantal responsibility by letting every member of the community contribute to halakhic interpretation and communal decision-making.\n\nIts impact on the expression of Judaism is just as significant, reshaping both ritual and marriage law. Progressive Judaism has held Bat Mitzvahs since 1922 in the United States, and Progressive and Conservative Judaism now permit women to join a minyan — the quorum of ten adults, traditionally men over thirteen, required for key communal prayers such as Kaddish — drawing on Deuteronomy 31:12's call for “men, women and children” to “hear and learn” the law together. Orthodox ritual participation remains more limited, but is opening incrementally: wearing a tallit is now a personal choice for Progressive and Conservative women, and a feminist reading of Numbers 15:37–41 argues that the tzitzit (tassel) commandment was likely intended for all Israelites, not only men — directly challenging a traditional male-centric interpretation — while women's prayer groups and Torah education have expanded even within Orthodox communities. Marriage law shows a similar pattern of change: tradition frames Jewish marriage as the man “acquiring” the woman (kiddushin), with only the husband legally able to grant a divorce (a get). Conservative Judaism has introduced more egalitarian rituals, such as both partners exchanging rings, even as the husband remains legally obligated to grant a get under civil divorce, while Progressive Judaism has moved furthest toward full legal equality in marriage. JOFA has specifically campaigned against the status of Agunot — women “chained” in marriage without a husband's consent to divorce — citing “You shall not oppress the stranger” (Exodus 22:21) as extending to gender justice; this campaign achieved a notable success after the 11 September 2001 attacks, when the relevant Jewish authority permitted some widowed Agunot to remarry despite their husbands' bodies never being recovered, showing the movement translating theological argument into genuine halakhic change.\n\nMost recently, Jewish feminist hermeneutics — the practice of re-reading and reinterpreting sacred texts through a gender-inclusive lens, recovering women's presence and voice within tradition rather than treating it as fixed — and the reclaiming of kol isha (“a woman's voice”, historically a restriction on women's public voice in some Orthodox contexts, now also invoked by Jewish feminists reclaiming that voice, especially through more inclusive scriptural language in the Progressive stream) show the movement continuing to inspire new forms of religious expression rather than having finished its work. Overall, Jewish Feminism's impact is comprehensive: it has changed who leads, how adherents worship, and how sacred texts themselves are read and understood, while remaining, at each stream's own pace and within each stream's own halakhic limits, a living development within the covenantal tradition rather than a movement outside or against it — precisely the kind of agency and enrichment the statement describes."
    }
