    exemplarAnswer: {
      label: "Model answer on bioethics (written by Claude, to the same real criteria)",
      text: "Judaism's ethical teachings guide adherents to navigate today's bioethical questions by weighing sacred texts against lived medical reality, always through the lens of two foundational teachings: Pikuach Nefesh (preservation of life, Talmud Yoma 85b) and Tzelem Elohim (being made in the image of God, Genesis 1:27 — a belief, not a mitzvah, worth stating precisely). Because “whoever destroys one life is as if he destroyed a whole world, and whoever preserves a life is as if he preserved the whole world” (Mishnah Sanhedrin 4:5), the starting instruction in every bioethical area is to “choose life, so that you and your descendants may live” (Deuteronomy 30:19) — but what this means in practice is shaped by lived experience and interpretation as much as by the bare text, exactly as the stimulus suggests.\n\nOn the start of life, Judaism's general position on abortion is grounded in Jeremiah 1:5 (“Before I formed you in the womb, I knew you…”), which affirms the sanctity of developing life from its earliest stages. Orthodox and Conservative Judaism generally restrict abortion after forty days except where maternal health is genuinely at risk, while Progressive Judaism allows broader consideration of factors such as mental health and personal autonomy — a real difference in how the same sacred text is read and applied. Rabbinic interpretation of the foetus as “mere water” in the first forty days (Yevamot 69b) makes abortion permissible in that period where maternal health is threatened; and where the foetus is a rodef — a precise term meaning a genuine, active threat to the mother's life, not merely a difficult pregnancy — abortion is not only permitted but required, across every stream. This shows adherents navigating a hard case by holding text and lived medical circumstance together, not by applying a single rule mechanically. IVF, by contrast, is widely permitted across the streams, since the wastage of unused embryos is not itself treated as an ethical obstacle; it fulfils the mitzvah to “be fruitful and multiply” (Genesis 1:28), a command that post-Holocaust Jewish communities in particular have emphasised, and the State of Israel's government funds IVF cycles until two live births are achieved, reflecting how a religious teaching and a national policy can reinforce one another in navigating a genuinely modern medical technology.\n\nOn the manipulation of life, Pikuach Nefesh and Tzelem Elohim are both applied with real force: stem cell research is considered permissible because it seeks treatments for life-threatening and life-limiting conditions such as Parkinson's disease, a position reinforced by Tikkun Olam (repairing the world) and Tzedakah (charity) and by the direct instruction “You shall not stand idly by the blood of your neighbour” (Leviticus 19:16). Indeed, Pikuach Nefesh is considered so significant within Judaism that it is understood to override almost all other mitzvot (Talmud Yoma 85b) — for example, Sabbath law may lawfully be broken where a life is genuinely at stake, showing that even Judaism's most central ritual obligations bend before the preservation of life. Organ donation illustrates how the same underlying belief plays out differently across the streams as medicine develops: Progressive and Conservative adherents generally encourage donation, including live kidney donation, explicitly citing Pikuach Nefesh; Orthodox adherents have traditionally been more cautious, concerned about defilement of the body given hopes of bodily resurrection, though this position is itself shifting, with some Orthodox rabbis now permitting post-mortem organ donation once death has been halachically confirmed — the Israeli National Transplant Center, for instance, actively promotes donation with rabbinical approval. This is Judaism adapting a genuinely ancient teaching to a technology the Torah's authors could never have anticipated.\n\nOn the end of life, Jewish belief holds that ultimately God decides when a person dies — “there is a time to be born and a time to die” (Ecclesiastes 3:2) — which grounds a general acceptance of mortality rather than a demand to prolong life at any cost. Suicide and voluntary euthanasia are clearly prohibited, resting on “You shall not kill” (Exodus 20:13); however, Orthodox rabbis commonly permit the passive withdrawal of life support, meaning not starting new treatment, while still forbidding any act that actively hastens death, whereas Progressive Judaism tends to permit broader patient autonomy, placing more weight on dignity and compassion in how death is approached. In every case the focus remains on the dying person, the gosses, being supported to die with dignity and to meet their Creator — a direct expression of Tzelem Elohim even at life's final moment.\n\nOverall, Jewish bioethics shows adherents navigating the modern world neither by rigidly following ancient rules regardless of context nor by discarding sacred text in favour of pure autonomy, but by holding textual authority and lived medical experience together at every stage of life, and adapting that balance differently across Orthodox, Conservative and Progressive streams as circumstances and medical possibilities change. This is precisely what the stimulus describes: ethical decisions shaped jointly by sacred sources and lived experience, helping adherents make sense of a rapidly changing medical world without abandoning the religious teachings that have always grounded Jewish ethics."
    }
