      text: "“We will do and hear everything the Lord has said” (Exodus 24:7) captures Judaism's continuing commitment to living out its covenant with God through action, not just words — and this is nowhere clearer than in the significant practice of Marriage and in the ethical teachings surrounding Bioethics, both of which show Judaism responding dynamically to evolving contexts while remaining anchored in sacred text.\n\nMarriage is a significant practice that allows adherents to reaffirm their faith and to express the ancient history and traditions of the Jewish people. Traditionally, marriage is framed through kiddushin, in which the groom formally “acquires” the bride, and only the husband is legally able to grant a divorce, known as a get — a structure that has remained largely intact within Orthodox Judaism. Conservative Judaism has introduced more egalitarian rituals, such as both partners exchanging rings during the ceremony, while the husband remains legally obligated to grant a get on civil divorce; Progressive and Masorti Judaism have moved furthest toward full legal equality, and in these streams same-sex marriage is accepted, reflecting the now-legal status of same-sex marriage in Australia. In these two streams, the role of women and their ability to take part fully in religious ritual reflects the egalitarian nature of contemporary society and changing societal norms. This evolution demonstrates Judaism's responsiveness to adherents' lived experiences, ensuring the religious tradition remains relevant and genuinely supportive of the people living within it, rather than a fixed relic of the past.\n\nMarriage also allows adherents to express core beliefs through its central symbols. The chuppah, the canopy under which the couple stands, symbolises the way God's presence is everywhere and the belief that the Jewish home should be open and welcoming to all, echoing the hospitality Abraham himself is said to have shown to strangers. The breaking of the glass at the end of the ceremony is a reminder of the fragility of life and a call to always remember the suffering of others — traditionally linked to the destruction of the Temple in Jerusalem — even amid joy and celebration, showing that even Judaism's most joyful practice carries an ethical seriousness at its core. Organisations within the Jewish community have also worked to address inequities within traditional marriage law: campaigns against the status of Agunot, women “chained” in marriage because a husband will not consent to a get, cite “You shall not oppress the stranger” (Exodus 22:21) as extending to gender justice within marriage itself — a reminder that Marriage as a living practice is not static even within more traditional streams.\n\nThe interpretation and implementation of bioethical teachings within Judaism further highlights the capacity of the different streams to address contemporary issues through renewed interpretation of sacred writings. The preservation of life has always been regarded as a cardinal value in Judaism — the Torah was given to humanity so “that he may live”, and Pikuach Nefesh (preservation of life, Talmud Yoma 85b) is considered so significant that it is understood to override almost all other mitzvot. Judaism also mandates that the value of a life is not dependent on the health, age or circumstances of the individual concerned, grounded in the belief that every person is made Tzelem Elohim, in the image of God (Genesis 1:27). Taking one's own life is regarded as halachically and morally improper; while Judaism cannot condemn those who, amid unbearable pain and suffering, take their own lives, it cannot encourage, condone or participate in the commission of such an act, since “You shall not kill” (Exodus 20:13) stands as a clear prohibition even where compassion for suffering is genuinely felt. At the same time, Orthodox rabbis commonly permit the passive withdrawal of life-prolonging treatment, while forbidding any act that actively hastens death, and Progressive Judaism generally places greater emphasis on patient autonomy and dignity in these decisions — again showing the same underlying value, the sanctity of life, interpreted with real variation across the streams as medicine and society change.\n\nMarriage and Bioethics may, at first glance, appear to be very different areas of Jewish life — one a joyful communal ritual, the other a set of difficult, often private, ethical decisions. Yet both are united by the same underlying pattern that defines Judaism as a living religious tradition: adherents returning again and again to sacred text and inherited law, and then applying it, adapting it and sometimes reworking its outward expression, in order to remain genuinely responsive to the lived reality of the Jewish community today. This is exactly what it means, as the stimulus puts it, to “do and hear everything the Lord has said” — not simply reciting the Law, but continuing to act it out in a changing world."
